oncos went 14-3 in 2025 and outscored opponents 401 to 311. Both sides of the ball were strong, with the offense 10th in EPA per play and the defense 6th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd highest screen rate and the highest man coverage rate in the league.","offense":"The Broncos offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest screen rate and a 22 personnel usage that was among the highest in the league. Bo Nix ran the passing game, with Courtland Sutton leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Broncos had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 6th or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 26th. Nik Bonitto was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was John Franklin-Myers, who had 7.5 sacks despite playing only 49% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest man coverage rate in the league.","special":"The Broncos were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 88% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the offensive line, about 27% of the money on the books. Their tight ends were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Bo Nix played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,931 yards. Targets were spread around, with Courtland Sutton and Troy Franklin leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by J.K. Dobbins and RJ Harvey. On defense, Nik Bonitto led the team with 14 sacks and Ja'Quan McMillian picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Sean Payton called the offense as head coach and Vance Joseph called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Chargers and the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Giants and the 2022 Chargers under Brandon Staley.","trends":"The offense went from 14th in EPA per play in 2024 to 10th in 2025, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 1st in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 6th in 2025, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 2% to 13%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}